>> title: Puerquitos
>> category: Cookies
>> tags: piloncillo, mexican, spiced, cookies, oven
>> counters: Panadería
>> aka: puerquito, puerquitos, marranito, marranitos, cochinito de piloncillo, cochinitos, pig cookies, gingerbread pigs
>> pairs-with: piloncillo-syrup
>> servings: 18
>> time: 1 hr 45 min
>> step.1: cream the fats and sugar
>> step.2: beat in the egg and the syrup
>> step.3: work in the dry, chill 1 hr
>> step.4: roll, cut 18 pigs, wash
>> step.5: bake at 350°F 12 min

Cream @vegetable shortening{1/2%cup}(95 g), @lard{1/4%cup}(50 g) and @granulated sugar{1/4%cup}(50 g) until light.

Beat @egg{1%large}(50 g) and @miel de piloncillo{3/4%cup}(180 mL) into @&(~1)creamed fat{}. The syrup is the sweetener and the colour both — a puerquito made with molasses and brown sugar is a gingerbread pig wearing the name.

Work @all-purpose flour{3 1/2%cup}(420 g), @baking soda{1%tsp}(5 g), @ground cinnamon{2%tsp}(5 g), @ground ginger{1%tsp}(2 g), @ground anise{1/2%tsp}(1 g) and @table salt{1/2%tsp}(3 g) into @&(~1)syrup mixture{}, then ~chill{1%hr}.

Roll @&(~1)dough{} 1/4 inch thick, cut 18 pigs with a floured cutter, lay them well apart and brush with @beaten egg{1%large}(50 g).

Bake @&(~1)cut pigs{} at 350°F for ~bake{12%min}. They should still give in the middle when they come out — a puerquito is soft and cakey, not a snap.
